From 0017a1b2c3d4e5f60718293a4b5c6d7e8f90a1b2c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Tue, 4 Aug 2026 18:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] CUDA/HIP: run the MoE shared expert as a ninth channel of the
 routed expert down projection

Laguna-XS-2.1 pairs 256 top-8 routed experts with an always-on shared expert.
At batch 1 the shared expert's down projection is its own launch over a
[512 -> 2048] Q6_K slab: 2048 workgroups moving 0.86 MB. Measured on gfx1201
that takes 8.43 us, about 102 GB/s, while the routed down projection - the
identical per-row work at eight times the channel count - sustains ~601 GB/s
in 11.44 us. The launch is memory LATENCY bound, not bandwidth bound:
effective bandwidth tracks resident wave count almost linearly, so no thread
geometry fixes it (the GGML_MMVQ_UNTRIM_BLOCKS sweep is flat, 5.371 vs 5.422
ms/token at 0 vs 4096). The only fix is more concurrent work in one launch.

The two projections read equally shaped, equally quantized weight slabs and
differ only in weight/activation/output base pointers, so the shared expert
becomes one extra channel appended to the routed mul_mat_id grid:

  - mmvq_extra_channel carries the three base pointers; mul_mat_vec_q gains a
    compile-time has_extra flag, so every existing instantiation folds the
    branch away and keeps its stock instruction sequence.
  - The extra channel is not id-selected: it takes channel_x = channel_y = 0
    and writes at the start of its own output tensor.
  - Every row keeps the thread geometry, k-block assignment, vec_dot sequence
    and reduction order it has in the separate launch, so the result is
    bit-identical, not merely equivalent.

Making the two nodes adjacent has to happen at graph construction. ggml-alloc
assigns buffers from node order, so a shared expert emitted after the routed
reduction gets its output aliased with one of that reduction's temporaries and
the backend-side hoist is correctly refused. build_moe_ffn therefore emits the
shared expert's down projection immediately after the routed one, and
laguna.cpp builds its gate/up/SwiGLU beforehand - a pure reordering of
independent work that reads only the layer input.

That reordering alone would cost more than it saves: it moves the shared
expert's gate/up pair onto llama.cpp's gate+up+GLU fused mmvq (512 workgroups
x 256 threads, 11.49 us) and off the grouped launch (1024 x 128, 4.33 us),
because try_fuse fires at the gate node before the GLU+quantize fold is ever
consulted. Since these launches are latency bound, halving the workgroup count
to double the threads is the wrong trade. ggml_cuda_prefer_group_over_glu_fuse
declines that fusion for a batch-1 dense gate/up pair over a shared activation,
and is consulted by BOTH the fusion decision and the grouping guard so the two
agree and the pair cannot fall through to two separate launches.

Measured on the R9700 (gfx1201), llama-bench tg128, five interleaved whole
process launches per arm with rotating order, median of per-round ratios:

  candidate vs series 0001-0016   1.03950  (+3.95 pct, min 1.03832 max 1.04343)
  no-op control vs series         0.99734  (-0.27 pct, min 0.99333 max 0.99901)

The control is the same binary with GGML_MOE_SHEXP_FUSE=0, which restores both
the stock graph layout and the stock fusion choice; it lands on the reference
within noise, which is what isolates the change.

Kernel level (rocprofv3, per decoded token): 5.419 -> 5.182 ms of kernel time
(-4.36 pct) and 897 -> 858 dispatches, exactly one removed per MoE layer. The
Q/K grouped launch (27/token) and the shared expert's grouped gate/up (39/token
at 4.98 us) are both intact - dispatch counts were checked per kernel group,
not only in aggregate, because an earlier arrangement looked neutral in kernel
time while silently costing 75 dispatches per token.

Perplexity over the fixed corpus is unchanged at 5.2611 for the reference, the
candidate and the control.

GGML_MOE_SHEXP_FUSE=0 disables graph layout and fusion together, for A/B on a
single binary.
